       When  reading from the _u_r_a_n_d_o_m source (GGRRNNDD__RRAANNDDOOMM is not set), ggeettrraann‐‐
       ddoomm() will block until the entropy pool has  been  initialized  (unless
       the  GGRRNNDD__NNOONNBBLLOOCCKK flag was specified).  If a request is made to read a
       large number of bytes (more than 256),  ggeettrraannddoomm()  will  block  until
       those  bytes  have been generated and transferred from kernel memory to
       _b_u_f.  When reading from the _r_a_n_d_o_m source (GGRRNNDD__RRAANNDDOOMM is set), ggeettrraann‐‐
       ddoomm() will block until some random bytes become available  (unless  the
       GGRRNNDD__NNOONNBBLLOOCCKK flag was specified).

       The  behavior  when a call to ggeettrraannddoomm() that is blocked while reading
       from the _u_r_a_n_d_o_m source is interrupted by a signal handler  depends  on
       the initialization state of the entropy buffer and on the request size,
       _s_i_z_e.   If the entropy is not yet initialized, then the call fails with
       the EEIINNTTRR error.  If the entropy pool has been initialized and the  re‐
       quest size is large (_s_i_z_e > 256), the call either succeeds, returning a
       partially filled buffer, or fails with the error EEIINNTTRR.  If the entropy
       pool  has been initialized and the request size is small (_s_i_z_e <= 256),
       then ggeettrraannddoomm() will not fail with EEIINNTTRR.  Instead, it will return all
       of the bytes that have been requested.

       When reading from the _r_a_n_d_o_m source, blocking requests of any size  can
       be  interrupted  by  a  signal  handler  (the call fails with the error
       EEIINNTTRR).

       Using ggeettrraannddoomm() to read small buffers (<= 256 bytes) from the _u_r_a_n_d_o_m
       source is the preferred mode of usage.

       The special treatment of small values of _s_i_z_e was designed for compati‐
       bility with OpenBSD’s ggeetteennttrrooppyy(3), which  is  nowadays  supported  by
       glibc.

       The  user  of ggeettrraannddoomm() _m_u_s_t always check the return value, to deter‐
       mine whether either an error occurred or  fewer  bytes  than  requested
       were returned.  In the case where GGRRNNDD__RRAANNDDOOMM is not specified and _s_i_z_e
       is  less  than  or equal to 256, a return of fewer bytes than requested
       should never happen, but the careful programmer  will  check  for  this
       anyway!

